Hi [~accountid:5d5a47614aeeb80c2cc0d00a] - getting back to this.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Privacy`s preliminary opinion is that it should be possible to inform our existing Bolt users in CA about the Hopp launch without opt-in consent. This is because we are effectively informing the user about a new service of Bolt, however, under a different brand (Hopp).

We must be very clear with the Bolt user that the service is provider by the Bolt local subsidiary in CA under new brand “Hopp”, and this is not a third party promotion.

I will kindly request you to prepare an example of an email informing our users about the Hopp launch taking the above into consideration. Please share here so we can assess and add our advice directly to the template to minimise risks.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#ffebe6}
Per our current assessment, it would be challenging to justify such an update as “operational” - *as such, we advise that* *the update must follow direct marketing rules, such as including an unsubscribe link. For riders, this could be categorised under soft opt-in category “Bolt products”.*

In case we want to frame the update as “operational”, we must refrain from any wording in the email which will encourage the user to try out Hopp - it will have to be a bland message. But even in this case, there remains risks of being considered marketing, as to be operational, the message must be necessary for the ongoing operation or use of a product or service - i.e. billing updates, or important security or legal notices.
{panel}

